Schutz (1986) showed that three interferometers of different orientations are capable of determining d_{L}, {\\cal M}^{\\prime}, \\Psi, T, \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{S}, and \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{B}. In contrast, a single LLI can measure only {\\cal A}\\equiv\\Theta/d_{L}, {\\cal M}^{\\prime}, T, and \\Psi (FC); thus, d_{L} cannot be determined directly since \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{S} and \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{B} are unknown. However, we know that \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{S} and \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{B} are each uniformly distributed on the sphere; consequently, the distribution of \\Theta(\\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{S},\\hat{\\mathbf{n}}_{B}) is also known. Thus, the observed distribution of {\\cal A} is related to the number density of binary systems on spheres of constant d_{L}. Similarly, the observed distribution of {\\cal M}^{\\prime} is connected to the number density on shells of constant z. With a physically plausible assumption regarding the evolution of the source distribution function with z, the joint distribution of observed inspiral events as a function of {\\cal M}^{\\prime} and {\\cal A} is sufficient to measure H_{0}, q_{0}, and otherwise test cosmological models.

